# License

**autovideo is source-available under a split license. Using it commercially is not free.**

| you are | what you may do | do you need a license? |
|---|---|---|
| An individual — personal projects, learning, hobby, content you make for yourself | Use, modify, publish videos, self-host | **No** |
| Open source, non-profit, education, research, or a government body | Use, modify, run internally, contribute | **No** |
| A company or individual, using it to produce **commercial, advertising, marketing, or client-facing work** | Use, modify | **Yes — this requires a paid commercial license** |

The grant for everything in the first two rows is the
[PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0](https://polyformproject.org/licenses/noncommercial/1.0.0/),
reproduced in full in [`LICENSES/PolyForm-Noncommercial-1.0.0.txt`](LICENSES/PolyForm-Noncommercial-1.0.0.txt)
and governed by [`TERMS.md`](TERMS.md).

Commercial use is licensed separately. See [COMMERCIAL.md](COMMERCIAL.md) for what counts as
commercial and how to obtain a license.

Required Notice:

> Copyright (c) 2026 autovideo contributors — free for non-commercial use; a paid license is
> required for commercial use. See https://github.com/hashimaziz/autovideo/blob/main/COMMERCIAL.md

## Why not just MIT?

MIT grants the right to use this commercially to *anyone*, including a company, with no
conditions and no obligation to do anything. It is the wrong licence for software whose purpose is
to be sold to exactly those companies, and it could not be changed later to require payment:
a licence once granted cannot be withdrawn from the version that carried it.

This split licence keeps the things that should be free genuinely free — a developer experimenting
on their own videos, a student, an open-source contributor, a nonprofit — and makes the thing that
should be paid genuinely payable. If you would rather everyone paid, that is a different licence,
and this is not it.
